<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Ottoman Egypt</h1><p class="st-meta">1517 CE – 1805 CE · capital Cairo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Egypt as an Ottoman province for nearly three centuries, ruled by pashas from Istanbul while the old Mamluk elite kept real power.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a> destroyed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marj-dabiq">Marj Dabiq</a> in 1516 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ridaniya">Ridaniya</a> in January 1517, then hanged the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuman-bay-ii">Tumanbay</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> became a province of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, governed under the law code issued by Suleiman's grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pargali-ibrahim-pasha">Ibrahim Pasha</a> in 1525.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A pasha appointed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> governed with regiments such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissaries</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> households re-emerged as beys holding key offices. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> supplied grain and tax revenue to Istanbul and controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and spice trade of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-azhar">Al-Azhar</a> remained the leading centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> learning. In 1768 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-bey-al-kabir">Ali Bey al-Kabir</a> briefly declared independence.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Bonaparte</a>'s invasion in 1798 and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-occupation-of-egypt">French occupation of Egypt</a> shattered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> power. In the turmoil after the French left, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt">Muhammad Ali</a> was recognised as governor in 1805, beginning <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/egypt-under-muhammad-ali">Egypt under Muhammad Ali</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ottoman-egypt.jpg" alt="Ottoman Egypt" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: TRAJAN 117 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Egypt_(1844%E2%80%931867).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1517 CE – 1805 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cairo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Selim I, Ali Bey al-Kabir</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Ottoman Turkish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Egypt</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Egypt Eyalet, Eyalet of Egypt</dd></dl></aside></div>
